  "text": "To solve this problem, we need to create a function that processes a string, ignoring case and punctuation, and counts the frequency of each word.\n\n### Solution\n\nHere is a function that does what we want:\n\n```python\ndef word_histogram(text):\n    word_histogram = {}\n    regex = r'\\b[a-zA-Z]+\\b'  # this regular expression only matches words\n    words = re.findall(regex, text.lower())\n    for word in words:\n        if word in word_histogram:\n            word_histogram[word] += 1\n        else:\n            word_histogram[word] = 1\n    return word_histogram\n```\n\n### Explanation of the Solution\nThis solution uses a regular expression to extract words from the string. Regular expressions can become quite powerful, but they can also be complex, especially for more complicated patterns. In this case, the pattern is simple, so it's efficient.\n\nFeatures of the code:\n- The code is efficient and uses a linear time algorithm to perform the operation.\n- The regular expression matches sequences of alphabetic characters, effectively ignoring punctuation.\n\nWith this implementation, the function should handle all the provided test cases correctly.",
